        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> What's that Sound?</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Rooster!</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; colspan="2""><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; colspan="3"">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Respond to simple oral requests.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow simple classroom instructions with visual cues to recognize familiar sounds (e.g., 'Point to the animal that makes the sound.').<br>Can respond to simple questions with sound effects or gestures (e.g., 'What sound is this?').<br>Can respond to simple classroom commands during listening activities (e.g., 'Sit down').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Stage & Description</h3></th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Activities & Teacher Actions</h3></th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Estimated Date and Time</h3></th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with an energetic "Good morning, everyone!" and claps hands. The teacher then starts making simple animal sounds (e.g., "Woof! Woof!") and asks, "What animal makes this sound?" while pointing to a picture of a dog. The teacher encourages students to mimic the sound. This engages prior knowledge about animal sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a 'cat' and says, "This is a cat. A cat says meow!" emphasizing the /m/ sound in 'meow'. The teacher models the sound and gesture (mimicking a cat). The teacher then shows a flashcard of a 'rooster' and says, "This is a rooster. A rooster says cock-a-doodle-doo!" emphasizing the /r/ sound. The teacher also introduces classroom commands like "Sit down" and "Stand up" using TPR.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a cat flashcard) "Does a cat say 'woof' or 'meow'?" (Expected: 'Meow' or pointing to mouth)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Making a rooster sound) "Is this a dog or a rooster?" (Expected: 'Rooster' or pointing to rooster flashcard)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "What's that Sound?")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, colorful animal flashcards (cat, dog, rooster, bird, cow, duck, sheep, horse, pig), audio recordings of corresponding animal sounds, short presentation slides with animal pictures and names. Genre: Informative/Descriptive, Register: Simple/Direct, Format: Visual and Auditory Input.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying and describing different animals based on their sounds, recognizing common animal sounds, and mimicking sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows flashcards one by one, plays the sound, and explicitly names the animal. "Listen! What sound is this? (Plays 'woof') It's a dog! The dog says woof!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks students to repeat the animal name and sound after each introduction (choral repetition).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Gist Question) The teacher asks: "Do you hear many animals?" (Encouraging a nod or 'Yes!').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(TPR) The teacher gives commands: "Point to the big cow!" or "Show me the small bird!" to check understanding of adjectives and animal names.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to the initial letter on the flashcard for 'Cat' (C) and 'Dog' (D) for initial phonemic awareness.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Following simple oral instructions and questions, recognizing animal sounds, using 'What sound is this?' and 'The [animal] says [sound]'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Point:</b> The teacher spreads out flashcards of all target animals (cat, dog, rooster, bird, cow, duck, sheep, horse, pig) on the floor or whiteboard. The teacher plays an animal sound audio (e.g., 'quack'). Students listen and physically point to the corresponding animal flashcard. The teacher then asks, "What sound is this?" and points to the duck, prompting "Quack! That's a duck!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Stand Up, Sit Down:</b> The teacher gives instructions based on sounds. "If you hear a 'woof', stand up! If you hear a 'meow', sit down!" The teacher plays a mix of sounds, ensuring students listen carefully and respond with the correct action. This incorporates 'bark' and 'meow' and simple commands.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Animal Sound Match:</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of 4-5 animals (e.g., cat, dog, rooster, cow). The teacher plays a sound and asks, "What sound is this?" Students listen and circle the correct animal picture on their worksheet. The teacher models with one example first. (e.g., plays 'moo', students circle the cow). This introduces 'moo' for cow.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "What's that Sound?")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Sound Detective Game" - a guided whole-class activity focusing on listening and responding with gestures or single words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher places a set of animal flashcards (cat, dog, rooster, bird, cow, duck, sheep, horse, pig) in a "mystery box" or bag.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher draws one flashcard without showing it to the students and makes the animal sound (e.g., "Oink! Oink!" for pig).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen carefully and try to guess. The teacher encourages responses by asking, "What sound is this?" and "What animal is it?" (Expected responses: "Pig!" or "Oink!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After a few guesses, the teacher pulls out the flashcard and says, "That's a pig!" using an exclamation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The activity continues with different animal sounds (e.g., 'bark', 'meow', 'quack'). The teacher also integrates adjectives: "Is it a big animal or a small animal?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher praises students for good listening and correct guesses.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during the "Sound Detective Game" and a "Listen & Respond" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in a final "Listen & Respond" activity. The teacher plays 3-4 distinct animal sounds (e.g., rooster, cow, duck). For each sound, students must either:
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Point to the correct animal flashcard among a small selection.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Respond with the animal's sound or name (e.g., "Moo!" or "Cow!").</li>
                    </ol>
                    The teacher also gives a simple command like "Sit down quietly" to check following instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identification:</b> Can accurately point to 3 out of 4 animals when hearing their sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Response:</b> Can mimic 2 out of 4 animal sounds or name them when prompted ("What sound is this?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Instruction Following:</b> Can follow simple classroom commands (e.g., 'Sit down').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Effort:</b> Participates actively in listening activities.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> "Thumbs up if you heard many animal sounds today! Thumbs down if it was hard to hear." "Show me happy face if you know the rooster sound, sad face if not."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite animal sound we heard today?" (Students can make the sound or point to the flashcard).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you show me a big animal sound and a small animal sound?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students were able to identify and respond to common animal sounds with visual cues. The TPR activities were highly engaging for Kinder. Some students needed more individual prompting to mimic sounds. The /r/ sound in 'rooster' and /m/ in 'meow' were generally well-produced after modeling. The commands "Sit down" and "Stand up" were easily followed. I will need to provide more varied audio inputs and perhaps incorporate movement verbs like 'jump' and 'run' in the next lesson's listening instructions to expand vocabulary and physical response.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will engage with simple texts and visuals to identify these same animals and their sounds in a story context, reinforcing the vocabulary introduced today and introducing basic sight words related to the animals.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>